#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124194 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/id #uuid "a2c947c7-9873-5137-91ca-4fe0b42e9bb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11021" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z = \\dfrac {\\sqrt {y+1}}{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/5b/5b534c95f0105645223836a1b44f38db3f14bf61425b6f53ee7815742a86a068.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, y \\geq -1, \\: y \\neq -x \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124195 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "42cbeba7-685e-5782-9695-9671d16b7f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-11022" :question/problem "Sketch the domain of $g(x,y)=\\dfrac{1}{\\ln(x+y)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n![](images/68/68197b31685c209b98416f8e137dca514d7fbbf382d400688d411fa633ffd3c0.svg)\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD = \\left \\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\, : \\, y \\gt -x, \\, y \\neq 1-x \\right \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we plot the corresponding domain. \n\n- Starting with $y> -x,$ we draw a dashed line $y=-x$ and shade the region *above* the line.\n\n- Then, we draw a dashed line $y=1-x$ through the shaded region.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6a/6aa1aaeadb177cf5a80bcb12a247725cb3396586e7516c3ba30b46b03a9d2612.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124196 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "59fa0287-0562-5cf2-8469-946a69dc8adf" :question/math-academy-id "e-11025" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = y^2 - \\log_3(x - 7).\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(10,3).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(10,3),$ we substitute $x = 10$ and $y = 3$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,3) & =({y}^{2}-{log}_{3}⁡(x-7)){|}_{(10,3)} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-{log}_{3}⁡(10-7) \\\\  & =9-{log}_{3}⁡(3) \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(10,3)=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124198 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "bdae9fe0-a491-531b-ac36-7a37d2c32f16" :question/math-academy-id "e-11029" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z= \\dfrac{\\ln (2x+y)}{\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive,\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y>0 \\\\ x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\big\\{(x, y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, 2x+y >0, \\: x > 0 \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df3e244c-ae04-56cb-8d2f-103fe2a767ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274357} {:answer/id #uuid "0401f3db-693c-55a6-a2a6-cdcb8f618641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563976} {:answer/id #uuid "56d57d91-2728-51bc-b291-e2245cc07a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564108} {:answer/id #uuid "b88c88cc-ef4e-5607-aff6-aeedbfce6369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564254} {:answer/id #uuid "cc963b79-b269-5331-848d-67f4ee353859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc963b79-b269-5331-848d-67f4ee353859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564289} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d3ee636-ed6b-5d29-ac3b-8e818ea5617f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8022d60c-ad02-52ea-a2bc-6bcc108f5f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-158179" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3e6ed38-eb22-5a68-b4e4-a818bbc05c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564220} {:answer/id #uuid "b121cf1a-7dc7-5909-a04d-7262bf0ba8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564246} {:answer/id #uuid "c7eee758-44ec-5173-b8d7-7b012b545dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564281} {:answer/id #uuid "d680f939-d4c5-5d9b-a170-a6f596fd8729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y\\ne 0,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564307} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d25956-37d7-5565-be7f-161a70ab6752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b121cf1a-7dc7-5909-a04d-7262bf0ba8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564246} :answer-field/id #uuid "421fe0aa-3a0a-5d77-af83-754300b22259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a09ec73-a8f5-5e32-98fd-165c3e60bda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158183" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y>0 \\\\ x-y\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x-y>0,\\,x-y\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c87b3c0-88bd-5bcd-8655-c1b7cc94de43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563988} {:answer/id #uuid "2e779e34-e7fe-559c-8a5e-d6acdf7c0dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne y,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564046} {:answer/id #uuid "809ffa9f-fc24-57db-8fe0-6f88da3c8f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564165} {:answer/id #uuid "90267910-6534-5e32-a7b2-2a165379570f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne y\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564193} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f55a63-715f-589c-a5b9-f12320546593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "809ffa9f-fc24-57db-8fe0-6f88da3c8f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564165} :answer-field/id #uuid "0495e322-ad63-52d2-84e8-e016c8d6367c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5fbae1b-fa0a-5638-bec4-5b64d04c9a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-158185" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nNotice that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x.$ Therefore, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0\\,⟹\\,x>y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c3c2367-c89a-5a63-909a-8ab545f01ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563987} {:answer/id #uuid "269efb88-268f-56aa-a516-6833ee078794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564025} {:answer/id #uuid "94603c8a-00da-5e0e-8705-b52b7dbbcde4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564199} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a12713-af0d-576a-8ce3-2bf1f61336d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564308} {:answer/id #uuid "fa533f6d-4ad6-5d08-9dfd-22ac37f25112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94603c8a-00da-5e0e-8705-b52b7dbbcde4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564199} :answer-field/id #uuid "e365395e-e193-5723-b80a-2293c4ae62a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a819c67-d4ea-5946-94af-b50fc8d8ad96" :question/math-academy-id "q-158186" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡y}{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5fb42b-48b0-5faa-b43e-a2cc7b0c3a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564045} {:answer/id #uuid "47325857-bade-5abe-940b-58d047f337df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564087} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6f7aeb-aa8b-5188-b690-7774d66d49cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564233} {:answer/id #uuid "be3d5d38-7205-5015-a8b8-93d91f6b0ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ne \\pm 1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564259} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a17952-6dd7-5e95-8f91-a446078ae611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5fb42b-48b0-5faa-b43e-a2cc7b0c3a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564045} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1449eb3-c226-5410-a948-814f6bd35c51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e70a4727-f486-57b6-aa40-dbf0dba8fb16" :question/math-academy-id "q-158191" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}>0 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-1<x<1 \\\\ y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1<x<1,\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b746c30-aae0-5a72-8f59-c56e220b909e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b18e5383da3d9cd336d8829713127c670449da207ecc9216789747ee55ad3db7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564037} {:answer/id #uuid "48fd464b-717b-50cf-9914-97377d2a6891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c1/c165cffbac8308d93d73db8323f2e89e6231f8661c0c010947861be46f0a0040.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564089} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3bfeec-8a70-54e7-b902-621589c010e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5b/5b10601e98e7b8f4aea176c5a0eea21e806c61ecc8bf9e09bc231d36c848494b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564115} {:answer/id #uuid "873bd759-67ad-5785-b300-e95e0d07fa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7b7973136f5add18c648db5589bab6e3f3a8f1f2544473274e37f28ab282b5d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564178} {:answer/id #uuid "921a2c76-2d53-53a6-a4cc-08b4036be5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d60f7727323089821783619f48be4d61146e463a5d694f0817276f0e8a0986b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "921a2c76-2d53-53a6-a4cc-08b4036be5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d60f7727323089821783619f48be4d61146e463a5d694f0817276f0e8a0986b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564197} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7edea41-50a3-50bd-89df-5921c02bca14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aca8e51f-98b0-5cbe-9d48-2cf6165eb100" :question/math-academy-id "q-158194" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $<$ sign, the curve is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<1$ and shade the region *inside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/59/59ac983361be7dd9a991e1cf134e7193256891a5587eab6bff5edf906fef3688.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeac4431-91c7-5505-8522-2946cd2d43cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/4453ab63ec12361c69cffbf76bd51d63a9ef985b54452f7f84e37ed7dee21c39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564236} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf6bb0c-4f48-5455-b7c2-33db2af70756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9c/9c2a0516ca58c52ccecadade60e966563b442f3112cce73cb16c1931c2087cd8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564291} {:answer/id #uuid "e69db1ea-c330-5536-8560-08f5b1e80164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/338809ca0a3fe4d02c3523ec5e34a8d07a00b297d9f468740bbd1800d9675941.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564331} {:answer/id #uuid "fb69bb38-f4d8-5144-bff4-908369a1e63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564373} {:answer/id #uuid "ff779dbe-e82e-5919-99da-f2d26cecb00a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b8fe923f727ca57a8a2cfc756b93c0ed522bdd9edcc06c8097bead979c8e4b15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb69bb38-f4d8-5144-bff4-908369a1e63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564373} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c62e6a-7044-5e8e-b3aa-7123ad96f32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f90f5dc8-4e9d-5e87-800a-708d8b32cd8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158196" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\ge 0\n$$\n\nSince the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\ge 0$ is true for all values of $x$ and $y,$ the domain of the function $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ee07ec-7db5-5819-9326-c35abfd13309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/02/02c770831bde33820dd4f616df872e22d1a1f9d2de4e49073043cbd4139408e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564023} {:answer/id #uuid "a52a1264-daf9-5585-a899-ca4955b26a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8fc352405dfa98fc84dc5fc60a41d111139058b7680b9c3b02ee988079b34fef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564222} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b08043-54bd-579b-a710-49e36d027167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/ec1f53deac4b4a4c29daf9ad8205b6283b09f9402ce4df20a46c47e268bcd374.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564248} {:answer/id #uuid "bb553729-44e6-50a1-8fbf-d734ae3b55c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/2058a5e83362c3a2b823169cab5044ea8c3756c6f99ad9f8d26234e9f3b4421d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564255} {:answer/id #uuid "fae94e29-660b-5543-bab0-87e8d67f1ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bbf7c2cd4d8b11cd078f08d7ca31d3e7f40f20db3ee25fbbe95134d051840cc0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52a1264-daf9-5585-a899-ca4955b26a0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8f/8fc352405dfa98fc84dc5fc60a41d111139058b7680b9c3b02ee988079b34fef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564222} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3261e01-b350-524c-a353-a2dfc7e7e037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28327fac-a614-5756-91bd-3fcab54fcda2" :question/math-academy-id "q-158199" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx-y>0\\,⟹\\,y<x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<x\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $>$ sign, the line is not part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a dashed line $y=x$ and shade the area *below* the line.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/77/77a1a03d037ee571fb0dc14ec8370c8b01f521b5a54798117c0b131000676599.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7dd88c6-a262-58f6-95e2-e5384e5a31d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/18dcabdd46f686f12ce0577d5398b525976ee8404209d24a187c3d372a3bf78a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378748} {:answer/id #uuid "e43659e6-8eca-521f-a3ac-7988fc36588b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c4add22572db01658a9c909f66be2d53f14331454ab32577ced33011fe05fafa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378749} {:answer/id #uuid "50b317ce-e6eb-5c10-bf7a-1fcf5464941b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c641990be320fa60b602e3d42ab5d29648eb3d5274645f8193018d132c964599.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378750} {:answer/id #uuid "04ab281c-1447-5ea1-a446-2b1264179bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e79d4b0590032bc951f56e3117a6e5048e7101e270043b3e01b11e961b8c52a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378751} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2d7682-8df8-5624-9a62-ae12d475ff4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e646fe749cc50c557df1a35c69d9de75b16064afe42e467e62784b705c189b36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b2d7682-8df8-5624-9a62-ae12d475ff4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e646fe749cc50c557df1a35c69d9de75b16064afe42e467e62784b705c189b36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378752} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0adcc51-cdb5-5e33-a2ed-45fc79d72d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f8af9ca-3fa0-5528-bb2b-5f736d2c06fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-158204" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{y+{x}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\ny+{x}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ge -{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\ge $ sign, the curve is part of the domain. Therefore, we draw a solid curve $y=-{x}^{2}$ and shade the area *outside* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b7a6dbbab890b469cf389fa27627e9c1a5f95d8892507fcd4ba5f70541936ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d569954b-a9bd-5e60-8d8d-dc21240f8373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121422} {:answer/id #uuid "31ab3514-afb9-55f0-b797-5d67fafbd447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121423} {:answer/id #uuid "50456efb-8153-58c0-b558-6f6aca1e4608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121424} {:answer/id #uuid "55fccb26-6f80-5c7f-a2da-3df8bb2dc868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121425} {:answer/id #uuid "69feff14-68ef-55b8-89be-19860c1c8bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69feff14-68ef-55b8-89be-19860c1c8bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121426} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a5c3de9-0c28-55d6-a0e6-dcc003a6afdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15761064-db02-546d-ba8e-469a8fb53fd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-158245" :question/problem "Evaluate $z={x}^{2}+xy-5$ at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(1,2),$ we substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(1,2)} & =({x}^{2}+xy-5)\\,{|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =(1)^{2}+(1)(2)-5 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3795c09d-83a0-5e77-8d46-905638f8cd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564063} {:answer/id #uuid "766255f4-6ead-5147-a37a-dead8da19ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564148} {:answer/id #uuid "afd5b1b0-fcf3-5a5d-8da1-bec2e7cc53ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564243} {:answer/id #uuid "cedcf912-6c17-5453-a419-96804f0a8e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}=1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564293} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a68aca-012d-5c87-957b-411dd32e2492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1a68aca-012d-5c87-957b-411dd32e2492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564323} :answer-field/id #uuid "90be3a6c-5de3-5d62-b8c6-33fbd612aecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e55ad1f-d84a-5ded-9fca-459293bc995a" :question/math-academy-id "q-158549" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eae026c-0425-5d9d-9d0d-2c5cc016d5a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276902} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa5338f-2040-50a2-8215-a65d278835dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564077} {:answer/id #uuid "5f64eacd-5b71-5fb7-a6a8-59aa2ee03f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564117} {:answer/id #uuid "802c8eca-f606-57bb-ba41-d622b1cfd092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564163} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a97e5b-e0be-5f92-8ef2-8ec7a7cfb42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne -1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fa5338f-2040-50a2-8215-a65d278835dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564077} :answer-field/id #uuid "df108a95-6e90-54f8-bc6b-d51e2e24412d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83b99b1c-0bfd-5266-a9a8-e11b90bb0b2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-158553" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2x+y\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,2x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acdc5a5-82d8-5e8c-8ed0-61de70bd1223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563986} {:answer/id #uuid "0f719873-855b-510a-a974-761504b7537d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+y\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563991} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd2fadc-384e-56c3-ae50-0d963c2dd45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564071} {:answer/id #uuid "76aded1e-0a67-55a3-a895-85e73d77a133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564149} {:answer/id #uuid "afcfd169-0a6a-5b23-9542-bc1a80e450be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd2fadc-384e-56c3-ae50-0d963c2dd45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564071} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0effc05-87d0-589c-bbda-8eebc905e53e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1824679-625d-5463-b2f3-fb72a493af82" :question/math-academy-id "q-158554" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124636 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e593685-f5ca-5501-94bf-7cb4d49e935b" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Involving Reciprocals and Radicals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "235f0230-c6a2-58fc-bd59-7672f354b6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564021} {:answer/id #uuid "aae1a469-d68e-59c0-a688-35b023ed0b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y={x}^{2},\\,y>2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564227} {:answer/id #uuid "aef90284-fc08-5b4f-9e81-9caa92dfe802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<{x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564239} {:answer/id #uuid "b015e9e9-ed69-58a1-bc47-3d84c2927025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y>2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564244} {:answer/id #uuid "c6cc232c-cc23-5849-87bd-c3d853e16f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy-$plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "235f0230-c6a2-58fc-bd59-7672f354b6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564021} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbec5fcc-3c3a-5ffd-b0f3-92a5d7992122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f0c6fb0-68b0-537c-ab8d-954592bf8ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158561" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{y-{x}^{2}}}{2x-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-{x}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ 2x-y\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge {x}^{2} \\\\ y\\ne 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge {x}^{2},\\,y\\ne 2x\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3790236b-105a-5b9e-9ebd-ef017e764624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564062} {:answer/id #uuid "6348d00d-0685-5ea8-9c47-f047fe568924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564124} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1679b6-2cd1-5f54-b5fb-e698dad4fee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564140} {:answer/id #uuid "88f4eee4-d9a2-56a3-9cd0-87f68eac7ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564182} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3293bb-1f94-5c8d-a23a-3cb06b1be4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6348d00d-0685-5ea8-9c47-f047fe568924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564124} :answer-field/id #uuid "10d9af3a-10f7-5e92-bc23-9dd627657054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be7cd3c6-b4d8-58fc-a0bb-f9185ad55778" :question/math-academy-id "q-158568" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y+1).\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-3,1),$ we substitute $x=-3$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3,1) & =x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(y+1)\\,{|}_{(-3,1)} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1+1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{arccos}⁡2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4332d078-c596-5ab9-a9fd-b2600b41dcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564083} {:answer/id #uuid "529e6e4c-543b-5b5d-91ba-42883dfabf92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564102} {:answer/id #uuid "ade04a22-b4eb-505b-8eb9-82420c1e8561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564234} {:answer/id #uuid "c11088d1-89b4-5f2e-9c65-45b33b86af64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564266} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a761eb-6b14-555e-ba52-76a27a6e6871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c11088d1-89b4-5f2e-9c65-45b33b86af64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564266} :answer-field/id #uuid "378dd559-16b2-53c2-b64a-808e9c526e35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "911398b5-8165-51fb-be3f-bfee0bb6f77f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158571" :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\frac{2y-x}{{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}-4}$ at the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(2,-2),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(2,-2)} & =\\frac{2y-x}{{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}-4}\\,{|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-2)-2}{{2}^{3}-(-2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-2}{8-4-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c76e3aa-4667-51f4-960b-54ca94e95bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276006} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9d4847-f4a3-5e51-8843-844c295abf04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276161} {:answer/id #uuid "547dc3c1-d89d-5b16-897a-edc2f16a48c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276228} {:answer/id #uuid "56bc4319-f696-511e-8032-dbec796ecaea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276251} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d39fd4-0a44-568e-b95b-3b8fe25d4342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c76e3aa-4667-51f4-960b-54ca94e95bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276006} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ad22a6-5ade-5693-8d0f-ccaadfcf6954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f50b49cb-5ece-5d2a-8bc6-eb00c81b1f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-158572" :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2y+4)$ at the point $(-4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-4,1),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(-4,1)} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2y+4)\\,{|}_{(-4,1)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(-4)-2(1)+4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-12-2+4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-10),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7a5bbc-3365-5bba-82b2-9e07c2d95446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276481} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2a0eb0-6434-58f7-9e6c-4792c1123498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564111} {:answer/id #uuid "9c51b544-d4a2-5932-8f15-bac18a3bfea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 0,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564208} {:answer/id #uuid "afb9bfc6-03a7-5955-a866-f8b80cc14adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564241} {:answer/id #uuid "c250b76c-84fb-5171-96ad-26f59f4cc182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c250b76c-84fb-5171-96ad-26f59f4cc182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564268} :answer-field/id #uuid "123bde27-b22e-5118-aaaf-2bd06d63606a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "806bcc75-b225-5d86-8c9e-f99567365362" :question/math-academy-id "q-158577" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0,\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124638 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9289c4d-4f7f-5704-a2cb-272f92d8fef3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ca77a3e-9085-58ef-87e4-a39c3ee58fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564188} {:answer/id #uuid "a69cfe99-5dda-5c7f-a8ae-e9e88f862d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564223} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a63f4b-3cca-51c6-b369-5d4f828546a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564272} {:answer/id #uuid "dbef1e38-02bf-5cd7-beba-116a24ac4bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564317} {:answer/id #uuid "e866b3d9-12d2-51c5-a482-af43a7c89490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69cfe99-5dda-5c7f-a8ae-e9e88f862d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564223} :answer-field/id #uuid "893376bc-f857-5c82-9b7f-472d1ddbccda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c34765f-7311-53ab-be9b-191b42487450" :question/math-academy-id "q-158585" :question/problem "Evaluate $z={2}^{-x}+\\frac{1}{y}$ at the point $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(2,2),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=2$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(2,2)} & =({2}^{-x}+\\frac{1}{y}){|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & ={2}^{-2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124637 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31993f67-cb13-54c1-825f-a871eb2dd965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/044cb99eef6bafea63a4b4dd8df2a465790ea8eda949d98dd97df2ed768791be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378831} {:answer/id #uuid "6e60753e-9691-518b-a34b-cefcf9d4bc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/93/934de8df0f2c2a0c93d3966a66f1eed69744079f4beee57d7cc46e6ca20fddcb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378832} {:answer/id #uuid "7ade9180-8b54-5d3a-bfdb-c1cb7778c73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2ddf4edfd91b14835e7b14ca8cebf08b82b8585d9fd18972c8b065d6153a62f0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378833} {:answer/id #uuid "501346e1-a1e0-553c-96ce-2d555eb1295a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/280ac039d18c0af132b0a2a85a3b94226c7ff2b310c460a1086d52402bc41865.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378834} {:answer/id #uuid "9eae9821-d4e8-5aa2-84b4-d170a1a95d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a111de19f5b6fdc447cac043e875b243c67709180ca23b2283d448cdbfda94d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31993f67-cb13-54c1-825f-a871eb2dd965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/044cb99eef6bafea63a4b4dd8df2a465790ea8eda949d98dd97df2ed768791be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378831} :answer-field/id #uuid "04cdf5f5-74c6-51ca-8422-d1cd0d51b9e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56f9c6d4-908f-5999-9300-6cd67e4208c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158600" :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+y\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,y\\ne -{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne -{x}^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the expression involves the $\\ne $ sign, the line is *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ and shade *above* and *below* the curve.\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/6c/6c85783dc6626a3fea9d06431ee31e202da8c4d88e2eb1c2b6608dccdc2b782e.png)"}]}
